# Rugged SOM release flow

## Quickstart

1. Check out the exact OpenWrt commit associated with the release. Download the
   `feeds.buildinfo` published with that release and use it as the feed
   configuration. Its entries pin every feed to the commit used for the
   release build:

   ```
   cp /path/to/release/feeds.buildinfo feeds.conf
   ./scripts/feeds update -a
   ```

   Do not replace this with an unpinned feed configuration or use a
   `feeds.buildinfo` from another release. If no release file is available,
   `feeds.conf.default` is only reproducible when every `src-git` entry ends in
   an explicit `^<commit>` revision.

2. Install symlinks for the packages from those pinned feeds:

   ```
   ./scripts/feeds install -a
   ```
3. Follow instructions [here](https://github.com/botblox/luci-theme-botblox/blob/master/README.md) to make custom BotBlox Luci theme available
4. To reproduce a BotBlox build configuration, apply 
   `bb-diffconfig-ruggedsom` for Rugged SOM, BB-SOM-B-1
   `bb-diffconfig-routercore` for RouterCore (top board), BB-RPMTOP-A-1
   instead of selecting options manually in `make menuconfig` (unless you are targeting a specific build)

   ```
   cp bb-diffconfig-ruggedsom .config
   make defconfig
   ```

   `bb-diffconfig-ruggedsom` is generated with `./scripts/diffconfig.sh` and captures the
   BotBlox-specific OpenWrt configuration delta. `make defconfig` expands this into a full `.config` with the correct defaults for this tree.

5. If you want to adjust after applying `bb-diffconfig-ruggedsom`, run `make menuconfig`. If you make changes and want to preserve them for future reproducible builds, regenerate
   diffconfig:

   ```
   ./scripts/diffconfig.sh > bb-diffconfig-ruggedsom
   ```

6. Run `make` to build your firmware. This will download all sources, build the
   cross-compile toolchain, and then cross-compile the GNU/Linux kernel & chosen
   applications.

## Manual upgrade (U-Boot + TFTP)

This method flashes the full eMMC image and will erase existing settings on the board.
We are working on a sysupgrade-based release later.

1. Build an eMMC image and locate the `openwrt-{release}-{version_code}-imx-cortexa7-botblox_ruggedsom-ext4-emmc.img` artifact. If you are building from source, search `bin/targets/imx/cortexa7`.
2. Copy the image into your TFTP server root (often `/var/lib/tftpboot/` or `/srv/tftp/` on Linux) and ensure a TFTP server is running. Please see [this example](https://docs.fedoraproject.org/en-US/fedora/f36/install-guide/advanced/Network_based_Installations/#pxe-tftpd) on Fedora Linux
3. Boot the board and interrupt autoboot to enter the U-Boot shell.
4. Load the image into RAM:

   ```
   env set serverip ${host_machine_ip}
   tftpboot ${loadaddr} openwrt-{release}-{version_code}-imx-cortexa7-botblox_ruggedsom-ext4-emmc.img
   ```

5. Calculate the block count of 512-byte blocks:

   ```
   setexpr blkcnt ${filesize} + 0x1ff
   setexpr blkcnt ${blkcnt} / 0x200
   ```

6. Flash to eMMC starting at LBA 0 to overwrite partitions 1 & 2 (boot & root):

   ```
   mmc write ${loadaddr} 0 ${blkcnt}
   ```

7. Reset the board and continue booting
   
   ```
   reset
   ```

## Installing a locally built kernel module (.ipk)

Kernel module packages must be built against the exact same kernel version/config as the firmware running on the board. We provide an OpenWRT SDK. You can also use our OpenWRT fork

1. Build the desired kernel module package in your OpenWrt tree:

   ```
   # build just the package you want
   make package/<feed>/<pkgname>/compile V=s
   ```

2. Locate the resulting `.ipk` under `bin/`:

   ```
   find bin -type f -name 'kmod-*.ipk'
   ```

3. Copy the `.ipk` to the board:

   ```
   # if connected to bridged lan port 1-4. Default IP address 192.168.2.1
   scp bin/.../kmod-foo_*.ipk root@192.168.2.1:/tmp/
   ```

4. Install it on the board:

   ```
   opkg install /tmp/kmod-foo_*.ipk
   ```

5. Load the module and verify:

   ```
   modprobe foo
   lsmod | grep foo
   dmesg | tail -n 50
   ```

## Release flow

We need to add the following to every release.

- u-boot.imx binary
- kernel binary (zImage) used in boot partition
- device tree blob used in boot partition
- rootfs cpio and tar archives
- partitioned image file containing uboot bootloader binary, boot partition (kernel/device tree blob) and rootfs

In future, we may simplify things by just using a FIT image.

# Routercore release flow

## Default login

Log in as `root` with the default password `botblox`. Change this password
immediately after the first login by running `passwd`.

## Changes made

Changes were made to the at91bootstrap SPL, uboot-at91, and linux packages to port to this board. 

### at91bootstrap

For this package, we ensured that
- Use FLEXCOM7_USART (CONFIG_CONSOLE_INDEX=8) for the debug output
- Make sure that debug console hardware init code `at91_dbgu_hw_init` enabled the FLEXCOM7 USART peripheral clock
- Made sure that the `uart_base` UART registers were set to the FLEXCOM7 UART registers
- We do not use PMECC autodetect using ONFI feature in NAND controller. This is because we already know the PMECC values for the NAND chip (rev A uses MT29F8G08ABAC variants)
with 4096KB page size, 224 spare size for OOB, 8 ECC bits, and 512KB sector size. 
- By hardcoding these values, we can create not just the at91bootstrap.bin but at91boostrap.bin.pmecc which has these values encoded in a header (0xc1e04e07) 52x. Means we can prepend this to other binaries to get a final flash image.
- We set the `CONFIG_IMG_SIZE=0x100000` and `CONFIG_IMG_ADDRESS="0x00040000"` to basically say load the binary at address 0x40000 with a size
0x100000 into `CONFIG_JUMP_ADDR="0x23F00000"` which is a RAM address. This is where uboot should go.

### uboot-at91

- Create out own routercore board with devicetree and defconfig.
- Make sure to set the `CFG_UART_BASE` to FLEXCOM7 again.
- Ensure that the DEBUG UART is set to FLEXCOM7 in `board_dbgu0_hw_init`.
- Assumed in at91bootstrap that the uboot will be flashed at 0x40000 + 0x100000 (size) so the 
primary uboot env environment sits at 0x140000. The secondary env at 0x180000.
- Make sure that the `CONFIG_ENV_RANGE=0x40000`, which is equal to the block size of the NAND chip (256KB).
- Correctly set the CONFIG_BOOTARGS to assume a NAND flash layout of 

0x000000  AT91Bootstrap with PMECC header
0x040000  U-Boot
0x140000  Primary environment, erased
0x180000  Redundant environment, erased
0x1c0000  FIT image (kernel + dtb)
0x800000  UBI root filesystem

- Ensure that the boot command uses `bootm` for a FIT file (.itb)

### linux-at91

- Device drivers for the MV88E6393X are enabled
- Include the device tree for routercore

